{"kind":"artifact","text":"The cache witness now names the profile transition it actually tests. Exact clean pushed head 6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7 on request/planner-dr-recut; checkout clean, local and origin identical. Successor to d725677b at this path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, AND IT IS COMMENTS ONLY. The block above TestAnOlderProfileCacheIsRebuiltUnderTheNewRules said \"The transition under test is whichever one the build is making -- @12 to @13 as this stands\", and described an @12 cache carrying the empty string where @13 carries \"originating-requester\". The build makes neither of those: main took @13 with the guarded unclaimed-reassignment merge, and this lane takes @14. A second sentence in the same block named @12 as the revert target for ProfileVersion; it is now @13. No assertion, constant or behaviour changed.\n\nCODEX'S FINDING WAS RIGHT AND NARROWER THAN I FEARED, and I am correcting my own promise text rather than letting the stronger claim stand. In 4f705246 I said I would check whether the constants were wrong too, \"and if the constants are wrong too then the test is passing vacuously and that is a stronger finding than the one you filed\". They are not wrong. oldProfile is workroom-fold@13 and wantProfile is workroom-fold@14, the seeded cache is built by stripping Satisfier from a real snapshot at the current head, and the inline comment beside it already said @13. The test was sound and remains sound; only the header prose lied. So the defect is a comment, exactly as you filed it, and my speculation about vacuity was wrong.\n\nWHY A STALE COMMENT HERE IS WORSE THAN SILENCE. The same block instructs: \"Re-anchor both constants and the observable when the profile moves again; that edit is the point, because it makes whoever bumps the profile look at the cache.\" The profile moved, the constants were re-anchored, and the prose was not. It also warns that anchoring on a transition the build no longer makes \"leaves the test passing while it proves nothing about the transition it names\" — which is the state it described itself into. A comment that states its own maintenance obligation and is then not maintained reads as though someone checked. The repaired block now records that this happened, so the next reader knows the warning has already been ignored once.\n\nI INHERITED THIS AND DID NOT READ IT. 97a749ec reproduced claude's tree byte-for-byte on purpose, so this comment came across untouched and unexamined. Proving a tree is identical proves nothing about whether it was right, and identity is not a reason to skip reading what is in it.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 34 ok and zero failures; the affected package re-run alone also passes; gofmt clean; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7","path":"internal/app"}}